            "text": "Morning light spills across the kitchen counter, turning dust motes into tiny suns. I stand with bare feet on cool tile and breathe, listening to the hum of the refrigerator, the distant clatter of a bus. Outside, a maple tree shakes silver leaves, a gentle tremor from the weight of dew. There is comfort in repetition, in the ritual of pouring coffee and reading a headline, even if the day ahead is uncertain. I imagine the city as a sleeping giant, already stirring with bicycle bells and dog walkers. Somewhere far away, the ocean ripples under a pink sky; somewhere here, a child giggles because a cardboard box became a spaceship. I am aware of the border between doubt and curiosity, and the way sunlight travels through a window at precisely the angle to warm my face. Words like \u201calways\u201d and \u201cnever\u201d feel too heavy for such moments. Instead I settle on the little verbs\u2014breathing, waking, reaching, letting go\u2014because they hold the rhythm of life. There is a quiet rebellion in choosing to notice rather than to scroll, to lay down a pen and simply watch the shadow of a leaf drift across the page. If the world is a collage of ordinary miracles, I will keep cataloging them, word by word, morning by morning. It feels like a pact with ancestors who traced constellations on cave walls, whispering that wonder survives the most ordinary days. I inhale, grateful for the promise of another small miracle. Again, I stay attentive."
